# The upstream reader's routes

### `apps/reader` in tutors-mono-repo

Tutors Ladder, week 8. Rung 5 of 7: Reader. Every path is at tutors-mono-repo `2b96b69`.

---

## Why look upstream this week

Rung 5 has five kinds of page. The real reader has many more, and a server. Looking upstream answers three
questions:

- Is the shape we learned (folder, `load`, service, component) the real shape? Yes.
- What does rung 5 leave out, and where does each thing arrive?
- How does upstream **specify** a reader? With the same EARS Rules you write in the lab.

---

## `apps/reader`, top level

```
apps/reader/
  package.json          svelte 5.57.2, @sveltejs/kit 3.1.1, "#lib/*": "./src/lib/*"
  vite.config.ts
  playwright.config.ts, playwright.static.config.ts
  src/
    app.css  app.html  hooks.client.ts  hooks.server.ts
    lib/                actor-data.ts  root-data.ts  server/
    routes/
  tests/e2e/            reading, resource-cards, accessibility, static-reader, ...
```

The app is thin. Almost every component lives in `packages/svelte/*`. That is next week.

---

## The route groups

| Group or folder | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `(course-reader)/` | `course`, `topic`, `lab`, `note`, `talk`, `video`, `wall`, `search`, `quiz`, `notebook`, `whiteboard`, ... |
| `(home)/` | the home page and the course list |
| `(auth)/` | sign-in pages |
| `(create)/` | the course creation wizard and check page |
| `api/`, `healthz/`, `metrics/`, `version/` | `+server.ts` endpoints |

Rung 5 mirrors `(course-reader)` with five kinds of page, and none of the endpoints: the Rung Fence fails on
any `*.server.ts`.

---

## How a course page is built

`docs/COURSE-PAGE-WALKTHROUGH.md`:

| Route | Service method | Component |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `/course/[courseid]` | `readCourse` | `structure/Composite.svelte` |
| `/topic/[courseid]/[...loid]` | `readTopic` | `structure/Composite.svelte` |
| `/lab/[courseid]/[...loid]` | `readLab` | `content/lab/Lab.svelte` |
| `/note/[courseid]/[...loid]` | `readLo` | `content/Note.svelte` |
| `/talk/[courseid]/[...loid]` | `readLo` | `content/talk/TalkAdobe`, `TalkClient` or `TalkMarp` |

> Every learning-object route in the reader has the same shape: a route folder, a `courseService` method,
> an index lookup, and one component from `@tutors/ui-components`.

---

## The lab route, side by side

Upstream, `(course-reader)/lab/[courseid]/[...loid]/+page.ts`:

```ts
const liveLab = await courseService.readLab(params.courseid, url.pathname, fetch);
const lastSegment = url.pathname.substring(url.pathname.lastIndexOf("/") + 1);
if (lastSegment.startsWith("book")) {
  liveLab.setFirstPageActive();
} else {
  liveLab.setActivePage(lastSegment);
}
```

Rung 5, the same file:

```ts
const { tree, found: liveLab } = found(await requestLab(params.courseid, url.pathname, fetch));
```

Upstream decides in the route, by the folder's name. Rung 5 decides in `readLab`, by asking the tree whether
the path is a lab (R-018). Same behaviour; one of them a scenario can reach.

---

## A route rung 5 does not have: the wall

`(course-reader)/wall/[type]/[courseid]/+page.ts`:

```ts
export const load: PageLoad = async ({ params, fetch }) => {
  const course = await courseService.readCourse(params.courseid, fetch);
  const los = await courseService.readWall(params.courseid, params.type, fetch);
  ...
  return { type: params.type, lo: course, los: los };
};
```

A wall is every learning object of one kind in a course: all the labs, all the talks. `createWalls` in
`packages/jsr/model/src/utils/course-utils.ts` builds them when the tree is decorated.

In this week's lab you specify this route for rung 5, then build it.

---

## Where the course comes from

Rung 5: an id is a folder under `static/courses/`, and nothing else (R-015).

Upstream, `determineCourseUrl` in `packages/svelte/course/src/course/services/lo-tree.ts`, specified by
`@rule-0659` in `tests/bdd/features/course/course-loading.feature`:

| Address | Fetched |
| --- | --- |
| `web-dev-101` | `https://web-dev-101.netlify.app/tutors.json` |
| `courses.example.edu/web/dev-101` | `https://courses.example.edu/web/dev-101/tutors.json` |
| `localhost:3000` | `http://localhost:3000/tutors.json` |

One reader, any course on any host. That is the contract paying off.

---

## Upstream specifies its reader the same way

`tests/bdd/features/course/course-navigation.feature`:

```gherkin
@rule-0005 @ears-event-driven
Rule: When a student follows a lab route, the reader shall show that lab with its first step and its
      breadcrumb trail.

  Scenario: Navigate from topic to lab
    When I follow the route "/lab/web-dev-101/topic-0/lab-0"
    Then I should arrive at the lab "First Web Page"
    And the first lab step should be rendered with the heading "Build First Web Page"
    And the breadcrumb should show "Web Development 101 > HTML Fundamentals > First Web Page"
```

Rung 5 splits this one Rule in two, R-018 (first step) and R-021 (breadcrumbs), so that each has one
"shall" and can fail on its own.

---

## A drift worth noticing

`docs/COURSE-PAGE-WALKTHROUGH.md` lists `apps/reader/src/routes/+layout.server.ts`.
At `2b96b69` that file does not exist. There is `+layout.ts`, whose comment says:

> The root load is universal, not a server load (Rule 0771): it runs in the browser, so no navigation asks
> for __data.json and the static build (SVELTEKIT_ADAPTER=static) needs no server.

Our `+layout.ts` makes the same choice, and the Rung Fence keeps it.

Documentation is L2, Told. The code and its tests are L3 and above. When they disagree, the code is right,
and the drift is a good first contribution.

---

## Running it

From the mono-repo root (`apps/reader/README.md`):

```bash
pnpm install
cp .env.example .env        # PUBLIC_ANON_MODE=TRUE: no backend needed
pnpm dev                    # builds ui-primitives, ui-navigators, ui-components, then the reader
```

Then open <http://localhost:5173/course/reference-course>.

- Needs Node 26.13 or later (`.nvmrc`) and pnpm 11.24 or later (`package.json`, `packageManager`).
- If you cannot run Node 26, read the files: the flow does not need a running server to follow.

---

## What rung 5 leaves for later

| Upstream has | Arrives |
| --- | --- |
| `sanitizeHtml`, sign-in, roles, locks | rung 6 (Guarded) |
| `adapter-node`, the container, release gates | rung 7 (Shipped) |
| courses from any host, search, walls, videos, quizzes, notebooks | the Summit (or your "Build beyond") |
| Tailwind, Skeleton, Iconify, markdown-it, Shiki, axe checks, presence, Supabase | the Summit |

Each line is something no rung 5 requirement asked for. Leaving it out is not a gap; it is pull.

---

## What to take away

- `apps/reader` is routes; each learning-object route has the same four parts.
- Upstream specifies loading and navigation as EARS Rules, as rung 5 does.
- Where rung 5 differs (404s, the decision moved out of the route), it differs so that a scenario can prove it.
- Docs drift. Read the code, and note the drift.
